target/
.idea/
*.iml

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPERATOR DATA — NEVER COMMIT. See DATA-GOV-1 in docs/BACKLOG.md §5.
# These trees hold real carrier CDR captures and their DECODED output, which
# carry subscriber identifiers. A push is irreversible: history, forks and
# caches keep content that is later deleted (the SEC-INCIDENT-1 lesson).
# Keep them on disk for local parity runs; they must not enter git.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
config/
data/

# The corpus is DENY-BY-DEFAULT: everything is ignored, README.md (the
# hand-written corpus contract) is the single exception. Enumerating known-bad
# extensions was the old rule and it leaked — a .parquet/.txt/.csv dropped in
# here was committable on 2026-07-31. Do not go back to a per-extension list;
# to track a new corpus file, add an explicit `!` line and say why.
#
# What this holds back, concretely:
#   *.jsonl              decoded records + rows (~37 MB across 25 files)
#   **/parity.json       ⚠ NOT aggregate — a 25-entry `content.examples[]` of
#                        {new, leaf, legacy}: actual decoded FIELD VALUES,
#                        incl. 15-digit subscriber ids and raw legacy bytes
#                        (verified 2026-07-30 across all 9)
#   PARITY.md            generated output; regenerable per corpus/README.md,
#   SUMMARY.json         goes stale silently, and enumerates carrier capture
#   **/report.json       filenames / network-element names
corpus/*
!corpus/README.md

# Archives of the above — a single `git add -A` would otherwise commit the whole
# corpus as one blob. This is how 132 MB nearly shipped on 2026-07-30.
*.7z
*.rar
*.zip
*.gz
*.tar
*.tgz

# Raw capture formats, wherever they land.
*.ber
*.dat
*.backup
